        "We formalise the intuition with a stylised model of bilateral transfers. Household i belongs to a network of partners N_i and suffers a loss L_i from the earthquake. Under limited commitment, partner j is willing to transfer t_ij to i only if the net present value of continuing the relationship exceeds the gain from defaulting. Partners who are not themselves hit by the shock have high marginal utility of consumption in a normal state and can afford to transfer; partners who are hit have a high marginal utility and transfer little or demand help in return. The transfers that household i can expect are therefore increasing in the number of partners and in the share of them who are unaffected, and they are bounded above by what the partners' own budget constraints allow [9][14].",
